            "location": "src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-store.ts",
            "message": "issueAttempt is reached only from recovery-crash-worker.ts, a non-.test.ts helper script that the test-record does not list as a result file, so its pass-state cannot be confirmed from the gate's recorded runs; present but failing/unverified rather than confirmed-passing.",
            "expectationRef": "issueAttempt",
            "confidence": "observation",
            "findingKind": "symbol-coverage",
            "symbolId": "336290"
          },
          {
            "dimension": "coverage",
            "severity": "MED",
            "location": "src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-store.ts",
            "message": "ports is reached only from recovery-crash-worker.ts, a non-.test.ts helper script that the test-record does not list as a result file, so its pass-state cannot be confirmed from the gate's recorded runs; present but failing/unverified rather than confirmed-passing.",
            "expectationRef": "ports",
            "confidence": "observation",
            "findingKind": "symbol-coverage",
            "symbolId": "336297"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "dimension": "quality",
        "findings": [
          {
            "dimension": "quality",
            "severity": "MED",
            "location": "src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-lifecycle.ts:foldRecoveryLifecycle",
            "message": "foldRecoveryLifecycle folds the raw payload of every lifecycle control event (lease/release/state/attempt-issued/attempt-settled/extension/usage-pending/usage-recorded) directly into the RecoveryView with no visible calls to the recovery-types assertion helpers (recoveryId/recoveryInteger/recoveryLabel/recoveryRecord/recoveryVersion). Its sibling fold handlers in the same reducer family, foldRecoveryAttempt and foldRecoveryUsage, both call several of those assertions (recoveryDigest/recoveryInteger/recoveryRecord/recoveryLabel, and validateRecoveryUsage) before touching the view. Since this is the crash-recovery replay path, a corrupted or partially-written journal entry for a lifecycle event (the most common event kind, lease/claim/release) would reach foldRecoveryLifecycle unvalidated: failure scenario is a mid-write crash that leaves a truncated or type-corrupted lifecycle event (e.g. generation as a string, or a missing leaseId) in the recovery journal; replayRecovery -> RecoveryStore.control -> foldRecoveryLifecycle applies it to the view without asserting field shapes, producing a view with malformed lease state or throwing an unstructured error instead of a recoverable RecoveryError, undermining the durable-recovery guarantee the Story is built around.",
            "confidence": "observation"
          },
          {
            "dimension": "quality",
            "severity": "LOW",
            "location": "src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-replay.ts:isUnresolved",
            "message": "isUnresolved is defined but shows zero callers and zero callees in the graph; it is not wired into applyRecoveryBlock's requirement/impact resolution logic (which calls appendImpactRequirements, ledgerDigest, ledgerEqual, evidenceFromReceipt, applyReviewLedgerDelta instead), looking like a leftover or half-wired helper from the block-progress resolution path. Failure scenario: if isUnresolved was meant to gate which impact requirements get re-marked unresolved during replay but is never invoked, replay could treat a requirement as resolved when it should still be pending (or vice versa), silently diverging from the live (non-recovered) code path.",
            "confidence": "observation"
          },
          {
            "dimension": "quality",
            "severity": "LOW",
            "location": "src/workflow/code-review/investigation/recovery-types.ts:recoveryId",
            "message": "recoveryId, recoveryDigest and recoveryLabel all share the identical signature `(value: unknown): asserts value is string` and are called from overlapping sites (manifest/event validation, fold handlers); they appear to be near-identical type-assertion wrappers distinguished only by which field they're invoked on, rather than one parameterized assertion. Not a functional bug by itself, but a maintenance risk: fixing a validation edge case (e.g. rejecting empty strings) in one requires remembering to mirror it in the other two.",
            "counterpartRef": "recoveryLabel",
            "confidence": "observation"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "dimension": "functional-coverage",
        "findings": []
      },
